<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Cell Biol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Cell Biology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0021-9525</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-8140</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4824294</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2109234</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>MITOCHONDRIAL PROTEIN SYNTHESIS IN HELA CELLS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Galper</surname><given-names>Jonas B.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1d90110N0x37d36a8">From the Department of Biochemistry, Albert Einstein College of Medicine, Bronx, New York 10461</aff><author-notes><fn><p>Dr. Galper's present address is the National Heart and Lung Institute, Bethesda, Maryland 20514.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>3</month><year>1974</year></pub-date><volume>60</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>755</fpage><lpage>763</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>30</day><month>10</month><year>1972</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>13</day><month>11</month><year>1973</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1974 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> HeLa cell mitochondrial proteins have been shown to be the products of two separate protein-synthesizing systems; one, the general cellular mechanism, sensitive to inhibition by cycloheximide, the other, a specific mitochondrial system subject to inhibition by low concentrations of chloramphenicol (Galper, J. B., and J. E. Darnell. 1971. <italic>J. Mol. Biol</italic> 57:363). Preliminary data have suggested that a mitochondrial <italic>N</italic>-formyl-methionyl-tRNA (f-Met-tRNA) might be the initiator tRNA in the latter (Galper, J. B., and J. E. Darnell. 1969. <italic>Biochem. Biophys. Res. Commun.</italic> 34:205; 1971. <italic>J. Mol. Biol.</italic> 57:363). It is demonstrated here that the synthesis of these endogenous mitochondrial proteins is also subject to inhibition by ethidium bromide and decays with a half-life of 1&#x000bd;&#x02013;2 h in cultures incubated with low concentrations of this dye. The role of formylated f-Met-tRNA as the initiator tRNA in the synthesis of mitochondrial proteins is supported by data from several experiments. The rates of ethidium bromide inhibition of both the charging of f-Met-tRNA and of the synthesis of mitochondrial proteins are strikingly similar. Inhibition by aminopterin of the formylation of f-Met-tRNA greatly depresses the rate of mitochondrial-specific protein synthesis. In the absence of the synthesis of these proteins, respiration, the levels of cytochromes <italic>a&#x02013;a</italic><sub>3</sub> and <italic>b</italic>, and the number of mitochondrial cristae are decreased. The implications of these findings as they relate to mitochondrial biogenesis are discussed. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>